<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- Compose owns all real theming (SakhiTheme, core:designsystem). These XML
         themes exist only for the manifest/window setup Compose can't cover:
         the launch theme (pre-Compose splash background) and a plain no-action-bar
         base so MainActivity's Compose content isn't drawn under a system ActionBar. -->
    <style name="Theme.Sakhi" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <!-- The parent theme's windowBackground is @android:color/background_light (white),
             which showed through as a hard white strip in the navigation-bar region once
             the bar itself was made transparent. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/sakhi_window_background</item>
        <!-- Belt and braces with MainActivity's runtime calls: MIUI re-applies its own
             opaque nav bar unless the theme also declares it transparent. -->
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
    </style>

    <!-- The launch theme, and the ONLY theme the manifest gives MainActivity. Handing the
         cold start to androidx's SplashScreen compat instead of painting a flat colour.

         What it replaces: this used to be `Theme.Sakhi` with a different windowBackground.
         That gave a cold start three separate visual states in a row — Android 12+ draws
         its own icon splash whether or not the app asks for one, then this flat colour
         window appeared while the process warmed, then Compose's first frame arrived. Three
         hard cuts is the "app pops in" jump, and no amount of Compose-side work can fix it,
         because two of the three happen before Compose exists.

         With `Theme.SplashScreen` there is ONE splash, it is the system's own, and
         `postSplashScreenTheme` swaps the activity over to the real theme the moment the
         splash is done — so the colour behind Compose never changes.

         windowSplashScreenBackground deliberately points at `sakhi_window_background`, the
         SAME colour the app window paints behind Compose (and which already carries a
         values-night variant), not at a splash-only colour. Splash, window and first frame
         are then the same value and nothing flashes between them. `sakhi_splash_background`
         is kept in colors.xml because it is the KMM-synced token; this theme just no longer
         needs a second, separate one.

         windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon is set explicitly rather than left to default. On
         API 31+ the platform would fall back to the launcher icon anyway, but below 31 the
         compat library draws nothing at all unless it is named, which would leave older
         devices with a blank coloured screen. -->
    <style name="Theme.Sakhi.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/sakhi_window_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/ic_launcher</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Sakhi</item>
    </style>
</resources>
